.TH DD 1 "September 2026" "Modular dd (Optimized Edition)" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dd \- convert and copy a file with dynamic autotuning, streaming hash and safety guards
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dd
[\fI\,OPERAND\/\fR]...
.br
.B dd
\fI\,OPTION\/\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Copy a file, converting and formatting according to the operands.
.PP
Unlike traditional implementations, this modular and optimized edition provides dynamic in-flight I/O autotuning, zero-copy fast paths, SIMD-accelerated transformations, hardware block-size awareness, streaming SHA-256 integrity verification, and target safety guardrails against accidental host system destruction.
.TP
\fBbs\fR=\fI\,BYTES\/\fR
Read and write up to \fI\,BYTES\/\fR bytes at a time (default: 512). Overrides \fBibs\fR and \fBobs\fR. If set to \fBauto\fR (\fBbs=auto\fR), the engine autonomously benchmarks and tunes the optimal block size during the first milliseconds of transfer.
.TP
\fBcbs\fR=\fI\,BYTES\/\fR
Convert \fI\,BYTES\/\fR bytes at a time.
.TP
\fBconv\fR=\fI\,CONVS\/\fR
Convert the file as per the comma separated symbol list.
.TP
\fBcount\fR=\fI\,N\/\fR
Copy only \fI\,N\/\fR input blocks.
.TP
\fBibs\fR=\fI\,BYTES\/\fR
Read up to \fI\,BYTES\/\fR bytes at a time (default: 512).
.TP
\fBif\fR=\fI\,FILE\/\fR
Read from \fI\,FILE\/\fR instead of standard input.
.TP
\fBiflag\fR=\fI\,FLAGS\/\fR
Read as per the comma separated symbol list.
.TP
\fBobs\fR=\fI\,BYTES\/\fR
Write \fI\,BYTES\/\fR bytes at a time (default: 512).
.TP
\fBof\fR=\fI\,FILE\/\fR
Write to \fI\,FILE\/\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
\fBoflag\fR=\fI\,FLAGS\/\fR
Write as per the comma separated symbol list.
.TP
\fBopt\fR=\fI\,FEATURE\/\fR
Modern optimizer feature switch (\fBauto\fR, \fBforce\fR, \fBsha256\fR, \fBasync\fR). Can be comma-separated (e.g. \fBopt=async,hash\fR).
.TP
\fBseek\fR=\fI\,N\/\fR
(or \fBoseek\fR=\fI\,N\/\fR) Skip \fI\,N\/\fR \fBobs\fR-sized output blocks.
.TP
\fBskip\fR=\fI\,N\/\fR
(or \fBiseek\fR=\fI\,N\/\fR) Skip \fI\,N\/\fR \fBibs\fR-sized input blocks.
.TP
\fBstatus\fR=\fI\,LEVEL\/\fR
The LEVEL of information to print to stderr:
\fBnone\fR suppresses everything but error messages;
\fBnoxfer\fR suppresses the final transfer statistics;
\fBprogress\fR shows periodic periodic transfer statistics.
.SH CONVERSION SYMBOLS
.TP
\fBascii\fR
Convert EBCDIC to ASCII.
.TP
\fBebcdic\fR
Convert ASCII to EBCDIC.
.TP
\fBibm\fR
Convert ASCII to alternate EBCDIC.
.TP
\fBblock\fR
Pad newline-terminated records with spaces to \fBcbs\fR-size.
.TP
\fBunblock\fR
Replace trailing spaces in \fBcbs\fR-size records with newline.
.TP
\fBlcase\fR
Change upper case to lower case (SIMD-accelerated on ASCII streams).
.TP
\fBucase\fR
Change lower case to upper case (SIMD-accelerated on ASCII streams).
.TP
\fBsparse\fR
Try to seek rather than write the output for NUL input blocks (vector-optimized).
.TP
\fBautotune\fR
Dynamically benchmark and select optimal block size while copying.
.TP
\fBsha256\fR, \fBhash\fR
Compute and print streaming SHA-256 digest on-the-fly without an extra I/O pass.
.TP
\fBforce\fR
Override target safety guard when writing to devices containing mounted system roots.
.TP
\fBasync\fR
Enable multi-threaded reader/writer double-buffering pipeline (asynchronous Ringbuffer).
.TP
\fBswab\fR
Swap every pair of input bytes.
.TP
\fBsync\fR
Pad every input block with NULs to \fBibs\fR-size.
.TP
\fBexcl\fR
Fail if the output file already exists.
.TP
\fBnocreat\fR
Do not create the output file.
.TP
\fBnotrunc\fR
Do not truncate the output file.
.TP
\fBnoerror\fR
Continue after read errors.
.TP
\fBfdatasync\fR
Physically write output file data before finishing.
.TP
\fBfsync\fR
Likewise, but also write metadata.
.SH FLAG SYMBOLS
.TP
\fBappend\fR
Append mode (makes sense only for output; \fBconv=notrunc\fR suggested).
.TP
\fBdirect\fR
Use direct I/O for data, bypassing the OS page cache.
.TP
\fBdirectory\fR
Fail unless a directory.
.TP
\fBdsync\fR
Use synchronized I/O for data.
.TP
\fBsync\fR
Likewise, but also for metadata.
.TP
\fBnonblock\fR
Use non-blocking I/O.
.TP
\fBnoatime\fR
Do not update access time.
.TP
\fBnocache\fR
Request to discard cache.
.TP
\fBnoctty\fR
Do not assign controlling terminal from file.
.TP
\fBnofollow\fR
Do not follow symlinks.
.TP
\fBcount_bytes\fR
Treat 'count=N' as a byte count (iflag only).
.TP
\fBskip_bytes\fR
Treat 'skip=N' as a byte count (iflag only).
.TP
\fBseek_bytes\fR
Treat 'seek=N' as a byte count (oflag only).
.TP
\fBforce\fR
Override target safety guard check against overwriting mounted system roots.
.TP
\fBasync\fR
Enable multi-threaded reader/writer double-buffering pipeline.
.SH TARGET SAFETY GUARD
When writing to raw block devices (\fBS_ISBLK\fR), \fBdd\fR cross-references the device number against active mounts in \fB/proc/mounts\fR. If the destination targets the host root (\fB/\fR), \fB/boot\fR, or \fB/home\fR filesystems, execution is aborted immediately to prevent fatal data loss ("Disk Destroyer syndrome").
To bypass this protection in emergency/rescue situations, supply \fBoflag=force\fR, \fBconv=force\fR, or \fBopt=force\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Flash a Linux OS image to a USB stick with dynamic throughput autotuning and immediate verification:
.RS
.B dd if=archlinux.iso of=/dev/sdb bs=auto conv=sha256 status=progress
.RE
.PP
Decompress and write an OS image on-the-fly via standard Unix pipeline:
.RS
.B zstd -dc archlinux.iso.zst | dd of=/dev/sdb bs=auto opt=async,hash status=progress
.RE
.PP
Stream raw disk backup to remote storage over SSH with multi-threaded double-buffering:
.RS
.B dd if=/dev/nvme0n1 bs=auto opt=async,hash | ssh root@nas "cat > /backup/system.raw"
.RE
.PP
Copy random test stream with 1 GB limit and exact byte counting:
.RS
.B dd if=/dev/urandom of=test.bin count=1G iflag=count_bytes bs=auto
.RE
.PP
Create a 10 GB sparse file:
.RS
.B dd if=/dev/zero of=sparse.img bs=1M seek=10240 count=0
.RE
.SH SIGNALS
Sending a \fBSIGUSR1\fR signal to a running \fBdd\fR process makes it print I/O statistics to standard error and resume copying.
